parent references. Possibly we can only remove nodes if the remaining tree still has required symbols. Also, we might have to consider that removing a node could affect children: if a parent is removable, removing it might also remove its descendants unless they are retained via other parents? But each node has only one parent. So if a node is removed, its entire subtree is gone. So we need to ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains (including possibly its ancestors). Also, the root is not removable, so it's always retained.\n\nWe need to examine the fixtures to infer expected outputs. The fixtures include three cases with similar structure. Let's compute for the first case:\n\nCase 0:\nNodes:\n- root: id root, parent null, symbol root, depth 0, removable false, utility 1.0\n- n1: parent root, symbol s1, depth 1, removable false, utility 0.339\n- n2: parent root, symbol s2, depth 1, removable true, utility 0.798\n- n3: parent root, symbol s0, depth 1, removable false, utility 0.124\n- n4: parent n2, symbol s1, depth 2, removable true, utility 0.428\n- n5: parent n3, symbol s2, depth 2, removable false, utility 0.428\n- n6: parent n1, symbol s0, depth 2, removable true, utility 0.721\n\nRequired symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"].\n\nWe need to cover root (always satisfied by root node), and s1. s1 nodes: n1 (symbol s1, not removable) and n4 (symbol s1, removable). So at least one s1 node must be retained. n1 is not removable, so it stays. So requirement satisfied regardless of n4 removal. We can remove n4 if we want. n2 is removable, symbol s2 (not required), removing n2 would remove n4 (but we might want to remove n4 anyway). n3 is not removable, symbol s0. n5 not removable, symbol s2. n6 removable, symbol s0 (not required). So we can remove any removable node as long as we don't break required symbols. Since n1 retains s1, we could remove n2, n4, n6. But n4 is child of n2, so if we remove n2, n4 is automatically removed. So possible removed nodes: n2, n4, n6. But are there constraints about children of removed nodes? If we remove n2, we lose n4 (which is removable anyway). So that's fine. Similarly n6 is leaf under n1, can be removed.\n\nThus expected output likely: removed_node_ids: [\"n2\", \"n4\", \"n6\"]? But let's check retained: all others: root, n1, n3, n5. But n5 is retained? Its parent n3 is not removable, so it stays.\n\nNow check case 1:\nNodes: similar structure, but utilities differ. Required symbols same.\n- root (root)\n- n1 (s1, removable false, utility 0.609)\n- n2 (s2, removable true, utility 0.796)\n- n3 (s0, removable false, utility 0.094)\n- n4 (s1, removable true, utility 0.825)\n- n5 (s2, removable false, utility 0.411)\n- n6 (s0, removable true, utility 0.359)\n\nAgain, n1 is non-removable s1, so we can remove n2, n4, n6. Same pattern.\n\nCase 2:\nn1 non-removable s1, n2 removable s2, n3 non-removable s0, n4 removable s1, n5 non-removable s2, n6 removable s0. Same.\n\nSo expected removed = all removable nodes? Possibly yes, because removing all removable nodes reduces tree while preserving required symbol s1 via n1. But we need to ensure that the utility? Maybe the objective is to maximize sum utility of retained nodes? If we remove nodes, we lose their utility. But we are not asked to maximize anything, just to output removed/retained. So likely it's simply: remove all removable nodes that are not necessary for covering required symbols. And in these cases, all removable nodes are not required, so remove all.\n\nBut we need to consider if there is a case where a removable node has a descendant that is the only node with a required symbol. In those cases we'd have to keep that node. In the given fixtures, each required symbol appears on at least one non-removable node (s1 on n1, root on root). So all removable can be removed.\n\nThus the algorithm: given the list of nodes, group by symbol? But need to consider tree hierarchy: if a node is removed, its whole subtree is