at are removable, then check if requirement satisfied. If not, we need to add back some nodes. That might be the intended logic: we want to minimize retained nodes (or maximize removed) while preserving requirements. So we can try to remove all removable; if the resulting tree (nodes not removed) still contains all required symbols, then done. Otherwise, we need to keep some removable nodes. The problem statement says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The oracle might be that required symbols must be present. So reduce as much as possible.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build dict id->node.\n- Build parent mapping and children list.\n- Determine initial removed set = all nodes where removable is True.\n- Check if after removing those nodes, for each required symbol, there is at least one node in the retained set (i.e., not in removed) that has that symbol.\n- If yes, done.\n- If not, we need to retain some of the removed nodes to satisfy symbols. This becomes a covering problem: we need to select a subset of removable nodes to retain such that each required symbol is covered. Since the tree is small, we can try to greedily add back nodes that are the \"lowest\" that cover missing symbols? In the given fixtures, no missing, so simple.\n\nBut maybe there's a more precise criterion: \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" might indicate that we can only delete nodes that are part of a valid \"reduction pattern\". The fixture doesn't provide any grammar. So likely the simple interpretation works.\n\nGiven the output schema, we just need to produce lists of node ids. So implement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # determine which nodes to remove: all removable nodes that, if removed, still leave required symbols.\n    # We'll compute subtree symbols for each node.\n    # Build tree and compute descendants recursively.\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in nodes}\n    roots = []\n    for nid, node in nodes.items():\n        pid = node['parent']\n        if pid is None:\n            roots.append(nid)\n        else:\n            children[pid].append(nid)\n    \n    # memoize subtree symbol sets\n    subtree_symbols = {}\n    def compute_subtree(nid):\n        if nid in subtree_symbols:\n            return subtree_symbols[nid]\n        sym_set = {nodes[nid]['symbol']}\n        for child in children[nid]:\n            sym_set |= compute_subtree(child)\n        subtree_symbols[nid] = sym_set\n        return sym_set\n    for nid in nodes:\n        compute_subtree(nid)\n    \n    # A node can be removed if after its removal, for each required symbol, there is still some node with that symbol outside its subtree.\n    # Since removals are independent (if we remove a node, we also remove its descendants), we need to consider that removing a node also removes its descendants. So for each required symbol, we check if there exists at least one node with that symbol not in the subtree of the node being considered. If yes, removal is safe for that symbol. If safe for all required symbols, we can remove.\n    # But note: if we remove multiple nodes, the condition might change. However, a safe set of removals is one where no required symbol is completely removed. We can compute the maximal set of removable nodes that are safe to remove simultaneously by checking condition for each node relative to the entire tree (before any removals). Because removals are nested, if two nodes are in different branches, they don't interfere. If one node is ancestor of another, removing the ancestor automatically removes the descendant, so we must remove the ancestor (if safe) and then descendant is automatically removed. So we can simply mark for removal all removable nodes for which the condition holds based on the original tree. Then after removing all such nodes, verify that all required symbols are still present (since condition ensures). But condition ensures that after removing that node's subtree, there is still at least one node for each required symbol outside. However, if two nodes' subtrees cover different parts, but together they might remove all nodes of a symbol? For example, required symbol appears in two disjoint subtrees; if we remove both subtrees, it could disappear. But condition for each node individually only checks that there is at least one node outside its own subtree. If both subtrees cover all nodes of that symbol, then each node's subtree contains some but not all? Actually, if symbol appears only in two subtrees A and B, and both are removable, then for node A, there is node B outside (so safe). For node B, there is node A outside (so safe). But if we remove both A and B, the symbol disappears. So we need to ensure pairwise removal doesn't cause loss. So we need to consider the combined effect. Therefore, we need to find a maximal set of removable nodes that can be removed together while preserving all required symbols. This is a combinatorial problem, but given small tree and fixture, unlikely to be needed. But we should handle it correctly.\n\nOne robust approach: start with all removable nodes as candidates for removal. Then iteratively check if after removing all currently marked nodes, each required symbol is still present. If not, we need to retain some nodes. We can greedily add back nodes that are the deepest that cover the missing symbols. Since the tree is small, we can brute force.\n\nBetter: The problem might be simpler: the required_symbols list indicates symbols that must be present in the final tree, and we should remove all removable nodes except those that are necessary to cover required symbols when no non-removable node provides that symbol. Given the fixtures, that algorithm yields all removable removed. So we can implement that.\n\nBut to be safe, we'll implement a general algorithm:\n\